from lib import Bank
b = Bank('quick-bio', 'bio', typed=True, seed=11)
M, W = b.M, b.W

T = 'Reproduction'
M(T, 'Which process produces genetically identical offspring from a single parent?', 'Asexual reproduction', ['Sexual reproduction', 'Meiosis', 'Fertilisation'], 'Asexual reproduction uses mitosis, so offspring are clones of the parent.')
M(T, 'In flowering plants, pollination is the transfer of pollen from', 'the anther to the stigma', ['the stigma to the ovary', 'the ovule to the anther', 'the style to the filament'], 'Pollen moves from the anther (male) to the stigma (female).')
M(T, 'Which hormone triggers ovulation in humans?', 'Luteinising hormone (LH)', ['Progesterone', 'Oestrogen', 'Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG)'], 'A surge of LH from the anterior pituitary causes the follicle to release the egg.')
M(T, 'Budding is a form of asexual reproduction seen in', 'yeast and hydra', ['ferns and mosses', 'bacteria only', 'mammals'], 'Yeast and hydra grow a bud that breaks off as a new individual.')
M(T, 'Binary fission is the main method of reproduction in', 'bacteria', ['fungi', 'flowering plants', 'insects'], 'Prokaryotes copy their circular chromosome and split in two.')
M(T, 'Which hormone maintains the uterine lining during early pregnancy?', 'Progesterone', ['Follicle-stimulating hormone', 'Testosterone', 'Oxytocin'], 'Progesterone keeps the endometrium thick so the embryo can implant and grow.')
M(T, 'An advantage of external fertilisation is that', 'many offspring can be produced with little energy per offspring', ['every egg is almost certain to be fertilised', 'the offspring are protected from predators', 'it works well on dry land'], 'Huge numbers of gametes are released; survival per egg is low but numbers make up for it.')
M(T, 'Spores are produced by', 'fungi', ['mammals', 'birds', 'reptiles'], 'Fungi (and ferns and mosses) reproduce with spores.')
M(T, 'Oxytocin is involved in', 'contractions of the uterus during birth', ['ovulation', 'sperm production', 'implantation'], 'Oxytocin stimulates the uterine muscle to contract during labour (and milk let-down).')
M(T, 'Which is an advantage of sexual reproduction?', 'It produces genetic variation in a population', ['It is faster than asexual reproduction', 'Only one parent is needed', 'Offspring are identical to the parent'], 'Variation helps a population survive a changing environment.')
W(T, 2, 'Compare internal and external fertilisation.', ['Both are the joining of a male and female gamete.', 'Internal fertilisation happens inside the female (e.g. mammals), so fewer gametes are needed and more fertilise successfully.', 'External fertilisation happens outside the body, usually in water (e.g. most fish), so huge numbers of gametes are released because most are lost.'],
  [('Gives a similarity AND a difference between the two, with an example or reason', 2), ('Describes one type of fertilisation correctly', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Outline the role of FSH in the female reproductive cycle.', ['FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone) is released by the anterior pituitary.', 'It stimulates follicles in the ovary to develop, and the growing follicle releases oestrogen.'],
  [('States where FSH comes from and that it makes follicles develop (with oestrogen release)', 2), ('States that FSH makes follicles develop', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how hormones control pregnancy and birth in humans.', ['After fertilisation the embryo releases hCG, which keeps the corpus luteum making progesterone and oestrogen.', 'Progesterone maintains the thickened uterine lining and stops the uterus contracting, so the embryo can implant and the pregnancy continues.', 'Later the placenta takes over making progesterone and oestrogen.', 'Near birth progesterone falls and oestrogen rises, making the uterus more sensitive to oxytocin.', 'Oxytocin from the posterior pituitary causes strong contractions; this is positive feedback, as contractions cause more oxytocin until the baby is born.'],
  [('Explains the roles of hCG, progesterone and oxytocin, and how they cause pregnancy to continue and birth to happen (positive feedback)', 4), ('Describes the roles of at least two hormones with some cause and effect', 3), ('Describes the role of one or two hormones', 2), ('Names a relevant hormone', 1)])

T = 'Cell Replication'
M(T, 'During which phase of the cell cycle is DNA replicated?', 'S phase of interphase', ['Prophase', 'Anaphase', 'Cytokinesis'], 'DNA is copied in the S (synthesis) phase, before mitosis begins.')
M(T, 'Meiosis produces', 'four genetically different haploid cells', ['two identical diploid cells', 'four identical diploid cells', 'two different haploid cells'], 'Two divisions halve the chromosome number and shuffle the alleles.')
M(T, 'Crossing over happens in', 'prophase I of meiosis', ['anaphase II of meiosis', 'metaphase of mitosis', 'S phase'], 'Homologous chromosomes pair and swap sections in prophase I.')
M(T, 'Independent assortment happens because', 'homologous pairs line up in random orientations at metaphase I', ['DNA is copied semi-conservatively', 'sister chromatids separate in anaphase II', 'crossing over joins chromatids'], 'Each pair lines up independently, so many combinations of chromosomes go into gametes.')
M(T, 'DNA replication is described as semi-conservative because', 'each new molecule has one original strand and one new strand', ['half the DNA is destroyed', 'only one strand is copied', 'the new molecules are made from half the nucleotides'], 'Each parent strand acts as a template and stays in one daughter molecule.')
M(T, 'Which enzyme joins new nucleotides to the growing DNA strand?', 'DNA polymerase', ['Helicase', 'RNA polymerase', 'Ligase only'], 'DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides; helicase unzips the helix.')
M(T, 'Which enzyme unwinds and separates the two DNA strands?', 'Helicase', ['DNA polymerase', 'Ligase', 'Amylase'], 'Helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between bases.')
M(T, 'A human body cell has 46 chromosomes. A human gamete has', '23 chromosomes', ['46 chromosomes', '92 chromosomes', '12 chromosomes'], 'Gametes are haploid: half the diploid number.')
M(T, 'Why must DNA be replicated accurately?', 'So daughter cells receive the same genetic instructions', ['So cells can divide faster', 'So gametes have half the DNA', 'So mutations are always beneficial'], 'Errors become mutations that may change proteins.')
M(T, 'Sister chromatids separate during', 'anaphase II of meiosis (and anaphase of mitosis)', ['anaphase I of meiosis', 'prophase I', 'interphase'], 'In anaphase I it is the homologous chromosomes that separate.')
W(T, 2, 'Outline how meiosis produces genetic variation.', ['Crossing over in prophase I swaps sections between homologous chromosomes, making new combinations of alleles.', 'Independent assortment in metaphase I lines each homologous pair up randomly, so gametes get different mixes of maternal and paternal chromosomes.'],
  [('Outlines crossing over AND independent assortment', 2), ('Outlines one source of variation', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Describe the process of DNA replication.', ['Helicase unwinds the double helix and separates the strands by breaking hydrogen bonds between bases.', 'DNA polymerase adds free nucleotides complementary to each template strand (A–T, C–G), giving two identical molecules, each with one old and one new strand.'],
  [('Describes unwinding/separating and building complementary strands, naming an enzyme or semi-conservative result', 2), ('Describes one correct feature', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain why it is important that meiosis halves the chromosome number, and how fertilisation and meiosis together produce variation.', ['Meiosis makes haploid gametes (23 chromosomes in humans).', 'When two gametes fuse at fertilisation the diploid number (46) is restored, so the chromosome number stays constant from generation to generation.', 'Variation comes from crossing over and independent assortment in meiosis, so every gamete is different.', 'Random fertilisation then combines any one sperm with any one egg, adding a further huge number of combinations.'],
  [('Explains constant chromosome number AND variation from both meiosis (two processes) and random fertilisation', 4), ('Explains chromosome number and one or two sources of variation', 3), ('Describes the halving of chromosome number or sources of variation', 2), ('Gives a relevant fact', 1)])

T = 'DNA and Polypeptide Synthesis'
M(T, 'Transcription produces', 'mRNA from a DNA template', ['a polypeptide from mRNA', 'DNA from mRNA', 'tRNA from amino acids'], 'Transcription copies a gene into mRNA in the nucleus.')
M(T, 'Translation takes place at the', 'ribosome', ['nucleus', 'Golgi body', 'mitochondrion'], 'Ribosomes read the mRNA codons and join amino acids.')
M(T, 'A codon is', 'three mRNA bases that code for one amino acid', ['a single base', 'three tRNA bases that bind DNA', 'one amino acid'], 'Triplets of bases on mRNA specify amino acids.')
M(T, 'Which base is found in RNA but not DNA?', 'Uracil', ['Thymine', 'Adenine', 'Guanine'], 'RNA uses uracil in place of thymine.')
M(T, 'The anticodon is found on', 'tRNA', ['mRNA', 'DNA', 'rRNA'], 'Each tRNA has an anticodon complementary to an mRNA codon and carries an amino acid.')
M(T, 'The DNA template strand reads TAC. The mRNA codon is', 'AUG', ['ATG', 'UAC', 'TAC'], 'Complementary pairing: T→A, A→U, C→G.')
M(T, 'In eukaryotes, introns are', 'removed from pre-mRNA before translation', ['the coding regions kept in mRNA', 'found only in prokaryotes', 'made of amino acids'], 'Introns are spliced out; exons are joined together.')
M(T, 'Which bond joins amino acids in a polypeptide?', 'Peptide bond', ['Hydrogen bond', 'Phosphodiester bond', 'Ionic bond'], 'Peptide bonds form between amino acids in translation.')
M(T, 'Gene expression can be affected by the environment because', 'environmental factors can switch genes on or off', ['the environment changes the DNA sequence every time', 'proteins are made without genes', 'genes are only expressed in gametes'], 'Factors such as temperature or chemicals can change which genes are transcribed.')
M(T, 'The shape of a protein depends mainly on', 'its sequence of amino acids', ['the number of ribosomes', 'the length of mRNA tail', 'the number of chromosomes'], 'Sequence (primary structure) determines how it folds.')
W(T, 2, 'Distinguish between transcription and translation.', ['Transcription makes an mRNA copy of a gene from DNA, in the nucleus.', 'Translation uses the mRNA code at a ribosome to join amino acids (brought by tRNA) into a polypeptide.'],
  [('Correctly describes both processes, with what each makes or where it happens', 2), ('Describes one process', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Outline the role of tRNA in polypeptide synthesis.', ['Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid.', 'Its anticodon pairs with a complementary mRNA codon at the ribosome, so the amino acids are added in the order the mRNA codes for.'],
  [('Outlines carrying an amino acid AND anticodon–codon pairing', 2), ('Gives one role', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how a sequence of DNA bases determines the structure of a protein.', ['The order of bases in a gene is transcribed into a complementary mRNA sequence.', 'The mRNA is read in triplets (codons) at the ribosome; each codon codes for one amino acid.', 'tRNA anticodons pair with the codons, so amino acids are joined by peptide bonds in the order set by the DNA.', 'The amino acid sequence determines how the polypeptide folds into its 3D shape, which decides the protein\'s function.'],
  [('Explains the link from base sequence to codons, amino acid order and folding/shape', 4), ('Describes transcription and translation linked to amino acid order', 3), ('Describes transcription or translation', 2), ('Gives a relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Genetic Variation'
M(T, 'A genotype of Bb is', 'heterozygous', ['homozygous dominant', 'homozygous recessive', 'a phenotype'], 'Two different alleles for the gene.')
M(T, 'Two heterozygous parents (Aa × Aa) are crossed. The expected phenotype ratio is', '3 dominant : 1 recessive', ['1 : 1', '1 : 2 : 1', 'all dominant'], 'Genotypes 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa give 3 showing the dominant trait.')
M(T, 'In codominance, a heterozygote', 'shows both alleles fully in its phenotype', ['shows a blend of the two phenotypes', 'shows only the dominant phenotype', 'cannot survive'], 'e.g. roan cattle show both red and white hairs.')
M(T, 'Red flowers (RR) crossed with white flowers (WW) give pink flowers. This is', 'incomplete dominance', ['codominance', 'sex linkage', 'multiple alleles'], 'The heterozygote is an intermediate blend.')
M(T, 'A sex-linked recessive trait is more common in males because', 'males have only one X chromosome', ['males have two X chromosomes', 'the Y chromosome carries the dominant allele', 'females cannot inherit X-linked genes'], 'One recessive allele on the single X is enough to show the trait.')
M(T, 'A test cross crosses an individual showing the dominant trait with', 'a homozygous recessive individual', ['a homozygous dominant individual', 'a heterozygous individual', 'itself'], 'Any recessive offspring show the unknown parent is heterozygous.')
M(T, 'Human ABO blood groups are an example of', 'multiple alleles with codominance', ['incomplete dominance only', 'sex linkage', 'polygenic inheritance only'], 'Three alleles (IA, IB, i); IA and IB are codominant.')
M(T, 'A pedigree shows two unaffected parents with an affected child. The trait is', 'recessive', ['dominant', 'Y-linked', 'always sex-linked'], 'Both parents must carry a hidden recessive allele.')
M(T, 'The phenotype of an organism is', 'its observable characteristics', ['its alleles only', 'its DNA sequence', 'its chromosome number'], 'Phenotype comes from genotype and environment.')
M(T, 'A colour-blind man (XᵇY) and a woman who is not a carrier (XᴮXᴮ) have a daughter. She will be', 'a carrier', ['colour-blind', 'unaffected and not a carrier', 'either, with equal chance'], 'She always gets his Xᵇ and a normal Xᴮ from her mother.')
W(T, 2, 'Distinguish between codominance and incomplete dominance, with an example of each.', ['Codominance: both alleles are fully expressed in the heterozygote, e.g. roan cattle with red and white hairs, or AB blood.', 'Incomplete dominance: the heterozygote shows a blend between the two, e.g. pink snapdragons from red and white.'],
  [('Correct distinction with an example of each', 2), ('Correct description of one', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'A tall pea plant of unknown genotype is crossed with a short plant (tt). Half the offspring are short. State the tall parent\'s genotype and explain.', ['The tall parent is Tt.', 'Half the offspring are tt, so the tall parent must have passed on a t allele to half of them; Tt × tt gives 1 Tt : 1 tt.'],
  [('Correct genotype with reasoning from the ratio', 2), ('Correct genotype', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Haemophilia is an X-linked recessive disorder. A carrier woman has children with an unaffected man. Use a Punnett square to find the chance of each kind of child, and explain why sons are more likely to be affected.', ['Mother XᴴXʰ, father XᴴY.', 'Gametes: mother Xᴴ or Xʰ; father Xᴴ or Y. Offspring: XᴴXᴴ, XᴴXʰ, XᴴY, XʰY, each 1/4.', 'Daughters: half unaffected, half carriers, none affected. Sons: half unaffected, half haemophiliac.', 'Sons get their only X from their mother; with no second X there is no dominant allele to mask the recessive one, so one copy causes the disorder.'],
  [('Correct genotypes, Punnett square, all outcomes and a clear explanation for sons', 4), ('Correct square and outcomes with partial explanation', 3), ('Correct parental genotypes with some correct offspring', 2), ('One correct genotype', 1)])

T = 'Inheritance Patterns in a Population'
M(T, 'The gene pool of a population is', 'all the alleles of all the genes in the population', ['all the individuals', 'the genotypes of one individual', 'the dominant alleles only'], 'Allele frequencies in the gene pool change over time in evolution.')
M(T, 'In a population of 100 people, 36 are aa. Assuming Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium, the frequency of a is', '0.6', ['0.36', '0.4', '0.64'], 'q² = 0.36 so q = 0.6.')
M(T, 'If q = 0.3, the frequency of heterozygotes (2pq) is', '0.42', ['0.21', '0.09', '0.49'], 'p = 0.7, 2 × 0.7 × 0.3 = 0.42.')
M(T, 'Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) are', 'single-base differences in DNA between individuals', ['whole chromosome changes', 'differences in protein number', 'copies of mitochondria'], 'SNPs are the most common type of genetic variation.')
M(T, 'Genetic drift has the greatest effect in', 'small populations', ['large populations', 'populations with no mutation', 'populations of bacteria only'], 'Chance events change allele frequencies more when numbers are small.')
M(T, 'Mitochondrial DNA is useful for tracing ancestry because it is', 'inherited only from the mother', ['inherited only from the father', 'the same in all humans', 'found in the nucleus'], 'mtDNA passes down the maternal line without recombination.')
M(T, 'DNA sequencing is used in population genetics to', 'compare allele frequencies and relationships between populations', ['make proteins', 'clone organisms', 'cut DNA at specific sites'], 'Large-scale sequencing reveals patterns of variation.')
M(T, 'Gene flow between populations', 'makes the populations more similar genetically', ['makes populations more different', 'creates new species quickly', 'only happens in plants'], 'Migration moves alleles between gene pools.')
M(T, 'The founder effect occurs when', 'a small group starts a new population with a limited set of alleles', ['a population becomes very large', 'mutation rates increase', 'a disease kills the oldest individuals'], 'Founder populations often have high frequencies of rare alleles.')
M(T, 'Which condition is NOT required for Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium?', 'Natural selection acting on the trait', ['Large population', 'Random mating', 'No migration'], 'Equilibrium needs NO selection, mutation or migration.')
W(T, 2, 'In a population, 16% of individuals show a recessive trait. Calculate the frequency of the dominant allele.', ['q² = 0.16, so q = 0.4.', 'p = 1 − q = 0.6.'],
  [('Correct q from q² and correct p = 0.6', 2), ('Correct q = 0.4', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Outline how DNA sequencing data can be used to study inheritance patterns in populations.', ['Sequencing the DNA of many people finds SNPs and other differences.', 'Comparing how often each variant appears in different groups shows allele frequencies, relatedness, migration history or links between alleles and diseases.'],
  [('Outlines sequencing to find variation AND how comparing it is used', 2), ('One relevant point', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how the founder effect and genetic drift can change allele frequencies in a population. Use an example.', ['Genetic drift is random change in allele frequency from one generation to the next due to chance; its effect is strongest in small populations.', 'In the founder effect, a few individuals start a new population, so its gene pool holds only their alleles, often at different frequencies from the original.', 'Example: the Amish of Pennsylvania descend from a small founder group and have a high frequency of Ellis–van Creveld syndrome.', 'Over generations, drift in the small population can fix or lose alleles regardless of whether they are useful.'],
  [('Explains drift and the founder effect with a correct example and why small populations matter', 4), ('Explains both with partial example or reasoning', 3), ('Describes one of the processes', 2), ('Gives a relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Mutation'
M(T, 'A mutagen is', 'an agent that increases the rate of mutation', ['a gene that controls mutation', 'a protein that repairs DNA', 'a type of mutation'], 'e.g. UV light, X-rays, some chemicals.')
M(T, 'A point mutation where one base replaces another is a', 'substitution', ['frameshift', 'deletion', 'insertion'], 'Substitutions change one codon only.')
M(T, 'Why are frameshift mutations usually more harmful than substitutions?', 'They change every codon after the mutation', ['They change only one amino acid', 'They never change the protein', 'They only occur in gametes'], 'Insertions or deletions shift the reading frame.')
M(T, 'A silent mutation', 'changes a codon but not the amino acid it codes for', ['deletes a gene', 'makes a stop codon', 'changes the reading frame'], 'The genetic code is redundant (degenerate).')
M(T, 'A nonsense mutation', 'creates an early stop codon', ['changes one amino acid to another', 'has no effect', 'adds a base'], 'The protein is cut short.')
M(T, 'Mutations passed on to offspring must occur in', 'germ-line cells', ['somatic cells', 'skin cells only', 'red blood cells'], 'Only gametes carry mutations to the next generation.')
M(T, 'Down syndrome is caused by', 'nondisjunction giving an extra chromosome 21', ['a point mutation', 'a deletion on the Y chromosome', 'crossing over'], 'Trisomy 21: three copies of chromosome 21.')
M(T, 'Which is an example of a chromosomal mutation?', 'Translocation', ['Substitution', 'Silent mutation', 'Missense mutation'], 'Translocation moves a chromosome segment to another chromosome.')
M(T, 'Non-coding DNA', 'includes regions that regulate when genes are expressed', ['never affects the phenotype', 'codes for every protein', 'is only found in prokaryotes'], 'Mutations in regulatory regions can change gene expression.')
M(T, 'Which mutagen causes thymine dimers in DNA?', 'UV radiation', ['Alcohol', 'Caffeine', 'Visible light'], 'UV links adjacent thymines, distorting the helix.')
W(T, 2, 'Compare somatic and germ-line mutations.', ['Both are changes to the DNA sequence.', 'Somatic mutations occur in body cells and affect only that individual (e.g. cancer); germ-line mutations occur in gametes or the cells that make them and can be passed to offspring.'],
  [('Similarity and difference including inheritance', 2), ('Describes one type', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Explain why a substitution mutation may have no effect on a protein.', ['The genetic code is degenerate: several codons code for the same amino acid.', 'If the new codon codes for the same amino acid (a silent mutation), the polypeptide is unchanged.'],
  [('Explains degeneracy leading to same amino acid', 2), ('States it may code for the same amino acid', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how mutations contribute to genetic variation and evolution in a population.', ['Mutations are the only source of new alleles.', 'Most are neutral or harmful, but some give a new allele that changes a protein and phenotype.', 'If the new phenotype gives an advantage in the environment, individuals with it survive and reproduce more (natural selection).', 'Over many generations the frequency of the useful allele rises in the gene pool, so the population evolves; this needs germ-line mutations, as only they are inherited.'],
  [('Explains new alleles, selection and change in allele frequency over generations, noting germ-line inheritance', 4), ('Explains mutation as new alleles and links to selection', 3), ('Describes mutation creating variation', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Biotechnology'
M(T, 'Which is an example of traditional biotechnology?', 'Using yeast to make bread', ['CRISPR gene editing', 'Making insulin in bacteria', 'Producing a transgenic crop'], 'People have used microbes in food for thousands of years.')
M(T, 'Selective breeding works by', 'choosing parents with desired traits to breed', ['inserting genes from another species', 'cloning cells in a lab', 'causing random mutations'], 'Over generations the desired traits become more common.')
M(T, 'A major concern with biotechnology in agriculture is', 'a reduction in genetic diversity', ['crops growing too slowly', 'too much variation in crops', 'crops being unable to photosynthesise'], 'Uniform crops are vulnerable to new diseases.')
M(T, 'Bioethics in biotechnology considers', 'whether the use of a technology is morally acceptable', ['only the cost of a technology', 'only the speed of a technology', 'only scientific accuracy'], 'Ethical, social and economic effects are weighed.')
M(T, 'Which is a likely future direction of biotechnology?', 'Personalised medicine based on a patient\'s genome', ['Ending all selective breeding', 'Replacing DNA with RNA in humans', 'Stopping the use of microorganisms'], 'Genome sequencing can tailor treatments.')
M(T, 'Insulin for diabetics is now mostly made by', 'genetically modified bacteria or yeast', ['extracting it from pig pancreases', 'chemical synthesis only', 'growing it in plants'], 'The human insulin gene is inserted into microbes.')
M(T, 'Biotechnology that changes the DNA of a crop could affect the economy by', 'raising yields but increasing reliance on seed companies', ['always lowering food prices with no costs', 'stopping all trade in crops', 'removing the need for farmers'], 'Benefits and costs fall on different groups.')
M(T, 'Fermentation by microbes is used to make', 'yoghurt and cheese', ['plastic bags', 'steel', 'glass'], 'Bacteria ferment lactose to lactic acid.')
W(T, 2, 'Outline one social and one economic implication of using biotechnology in agriculture.', ['Social: some people do not trust GM food, and labelling and choice become issues.', 'Economic: higher yields and less pesticide can cut costs, but farmers may have to buy patented seed every year.'],
  [('One correct social AND one correct economic implication', 2), ('One implication', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Describe how selective breeding has changed a named organism.', ['Wild cabbage (Brassica oleracea) has been bred into broccoli, cauliflower, kale and cabbage.', 'Farmers repeatedly chose plants with enlarged flower heads, leaves or buds as parents, so those traits became exaggerated.'],
  [('Names an organism and describes repeated selection of parents with a trait', 2), ('Describes selective breeding generally', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Evaluate the use of biotechnology to produce human insulin.', ['Before biotechnology, insulin came from pig and cow pancreases; supply was limited and some patients reacted to it.', 'Now the human insulin gene is inserted into bacteria or yeast, which make identical human insulin in large fermenters.', 'Benefits: unlimited, cheap, consistent supply; no allergic reactions to animal proteins; no animal slaughter.', 'Limitations: requires expensive facilities and regulation; some ethical concerns about GM organisms, though they are contained.', 'Judgement: the benefits to millions of diabetics far outweigh the small risks, so it is a highly successful use of biotechnology.'],
  [('Describes the process, gives benefits AND limitations, and makes a supported judgement', 4), ('Describes benefits and limitations with an implied judgement', 3), ('Describes the process or benefits', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Genetic Technologies'
M(T, 'Restriction enzymes', 'cut DNA at specific base sequences', ['join DNA fragments together', 'copy DNA', 'translate mRNA'], 'They often leave sticky ends.')
M(T, 'DNA ligase', 'joins DNA fragments by forming phosphodiester bonds', ['cuts DNA at specific sites', 'separates DNA by size', 'unwinds DNA'], 'Ligase seals the sugar-phosphate backbone.')
M(T, 'Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by', 'size', ['colour', 'base sequence only', 'number of genes'], 'Smaller fragments move further through the gel toward the positive electrode.')
M(T, 'PCR is used to', 'make many copies of a DNA sequence', ['cut DNA into fragments', 'insert genes into bacteria', 'translate genes'], 'Cycles of heating and cooling double the DNA each time.')
M(T, 'In PCR, the mixture is heated to about 95 °C to', 'separate the DNA strands', ['let primers bind', 'activate DNA ligase', 'stop the reaction'], 'High temperature breaks the hydrogen bonds; primers anneal at ~55 °C.')
M(T, 'A transgenic organism', 'contains a gene from another species', ['is an exact copy of another organism', 'has been selectively bred', 'has no DNA changes'], 'e.g. Bt cotton contains a bacterial gene.')
M(T, 'Plasmids are useful in genetic engineering because', 'they can carry a gene into bacteria and be copied', ['they cut DNA', 'they are found in all human cells', 'they are made of protein'], 'Plasmids act as vectors.')
M(T, 'Reproductive cloning of a mammal (like Dolly the sheep) uses', 'somatic cell nuclear transfer', ['PCR', 'gel electrophoresis', 'artificial pollination'], 'A body-cell nucleus is put into an egg with its nucleus removed.')
M(T, 'Whole-organism cloning reduces', 'genetic diversity', ['the cost of farming to zero', 'mutation in body cells', 'the number of chromosomes'], 'Clones are genetically identical.')
M(T, 'CRISPR-Cas9 is used to', 'edit a specific DNA sequence', ['copy whole chromosomes', 'separate DNA by size', 'make mRNA from DNA'], 'A guide RNA leads Cas9 to cut a target site.')
W(T, 2, 'Outline the steps of the polymerase chain reaction (PCR).', ['Denaturation: heat to about 95 °C to separate the DNA strands.', 'Annealing: cool to about 55 °C so primers bind; extension: about 72 °C, where Taq polymerase builds new strands. The cycle repeats, doubling the DNA each time.'],
  [('Outlines all three steps with temperatures or purposes', 2), ('Outlines one or two steps', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Describe how a gene can be inserted into a bacterial plasmid.', ['The gene and plasmid are cut with the same restriction enzyme, giving matching sticky ends.', 'They are mixed so the sticky ends pair, DNA ligase joins them, and the recombinant plasmid is taken up by bacteria.'],
  [('Same restriction enzyme, sticky ends and ligase', 2), ('One correct step', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Evaluate the effect of genetic technologies on the biodiversity of agriculture.', ['Genetic technologies such as transgenic crops and cloning let farmers grow plants or animals with chosen traits, e.g. Bt cotton or herbicide-tolerant canola.', 'They can increase yields and reduce pesticide use.', 'But large areas are planted with one genetically uniform variety and many traditional varieties are abandoned, so genetic diversity falls.', 'Low diversity makes crops vulnerable: one new pest or disease could destroy a whole crop, as in the Irish potato famine. Genes may also spread to wild relatives.', 'Judgement: they reduce agricultural biodiversity, so seed banks and keeping varied lines are needed to balance their benefits.'],
  [('Explains effects on diversity with examples, positives and negatives, and a judgement', 4), ('Explains the reduction in diversity with an example', 3), ('Describes a genetic technology and an effect', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Causes of Infectious Disease'
M(T, 'Which pathogen is a prion?', 'A misfolded protein', ['A single-celled eukaryote', 'A bacterium without a cell wall', 'A fungus'], 'Prions cause diseases such as CJD.')
M(T, 'Malaria is caused by', 'a protozoan (Plasmodium)', ['a virus', 'a bacterium', 'a fungus'], 'Spread by Anopheles mosquitoes.')
M(T, 'Viruses differ from bacteria because viruses', 'can only reproduce inside host cells', ['have a nucleus', 'have ribosomes', 'are killed by antibiotics'], 'Viruses use the host\'s machinery.')
M(T, 'Koch\'s postulates are used to', 'show that a particular microbe causes a disease', ['grow viruses on agar', 'make vaccines', 'measure body temperature'], 'Isolate, culture, infect and re-isolate the microbe.')
M(T, 'Pasteur\'s swan-neck flask experiment disproved', 'spontaneous generation', ['germ theory', 'natural selection', 'cell theory'], 'Broth stayed clear while microbes were trapped in the neck.')
M(T, 'Tinea (athlete\'s foot) is caused by', 'a fungus', ['a virus', 'a bacterium', 'a prion'], 'Fungi grow in warm, moist skin.')
M(T, 'A disease that can spread from animals to humans is called', 'zoonotic', ['endemic', 'non-infectious', 'genetic'], 'e.g. Hendra virus from bats via horses.')
M(T, 'Which is a plant disease caused by a pathogen?', 'Myrtle rust', ['Scurvy', 'Diabetes', 'Down syndrome'], 'Myrtle rust is a fungal disease of Myrtaceae plants.')
M(T, 'Cholera is mainly spread by', 'contaminated water', ['mosquito bites', 'touching a sick plant', 'airborne spores only'], 'Vibrio cholerae lives in faecally contaminated water.')
M(T, 'A vector is', 'an organism that carries a pathogen between hosts', ['the pathogen itself', 'an antibody', 'a vaccine'], 'e.g. mosquitoes, ticks, fleas.')
W(T, 2, 'Outline how Koch\'s postulates are used to identify a pathogen.', ['The microbe must be found in all diseased hosts and isolated and grown in pure culture.', 'The cultured microbe must cause the disease in a healthy host, and then be re-isolated from that host and shown to be the same microbe.'],
  [('Outlines the postulates in order (isolate, culture, infect, re-isolate)', 2), ('Gives one or two postulates', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Compare bacteria and viruses as pathogens.', ['Both cause infectious disease and can spread between hosts.', 'Bacteria are living prokaryotic cells that reproduce by binary fission and can be treated with antibiotics; viruses are non-cellular, need a host cell to reproduce and are not affected by antibiotics.'],
  [('Similarity and at least one clear difference', 2), ('One correct point', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how a named pathogen is transmitted and how its transmission can be reduced.', ['Example: malaria, caused by Plasmodium.', 'It is transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes: a mosquito picks up the parasite in blood from an infected person and passes it into the next person it bites.', 'Reducing transmission: insecticide-treated bed nets stop bites at night; draining standing water removes breeding sites; insecticide spraying kills adult mosquitoes.', 'Treating infected people with antimalarial drugs reduces the reservoir of parasites mosquitoes can pick up.'],
  [('Names a pathogen, explains its transmission and links at least two control measures to breaking transmission', 4), ('Explains transmission and gives control measures', 3), ('Describes transmission or control', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Responses to Pathogens'
M(T, 'Which is a physical barrier in plants?', 'A thick waxy cuticle', ['Antibodies', 'Phagocytes', 'Memory cells'], 'The cuticle stops pathogens entering leaves.')
M(T, 'Skin protects against pathogens mainly by', 'forming a physical barrier', ['making antibodies', 'killing infected cells directly', 'producing histamine'], 'Intact skin stops entry; sweat and sebum also help.')
M(T, 'Lysozyme in tears and saliva', 'breaks down bacterial cell walls', ['traps dust', 'makes antibodies', 'raises body temperature'], 'It is a chemical barrier.')
M(T, 'Cilia in the trachea', 'sweep mucus carrying trapped pathogens upward', ['kill viruses directly', 'release antibodies', 'digest bacteria'], 'Mucus and cilia form the mucociliary escalator.')
M(T, 'Inflammation causes', 'blood vessels to dilate and become leaky', ['blood vessels to close', 'fewer white blood cells to arrive', 'antibodies to be destroyed'], 'More blood, fluid and phagocytes reach the site.')
M(T, 'Phagocytes destroy pathogens by', 'engulfing and digesting them', ['making antibodies', 'releasing hormones', 'cutting DNA'], 'Phagocytosis is part of the innate response.')
M(T, 'Some plants respond to infection by', 'sealing off infected tissue and producing toxic chemicals', ['making antibodies', 'producing T cells', 'raising their temperature'], 'e.g. abscission, cell death and phytoalexins.')
M(T, 'Fever helps fight infection because', 'higher temperatures slow pathogen reproduction and speed up immune responses', ['it kills all body cells', 'it stops antibody production', 'it lowers metabolic rate'], 'A moderate fever is a defence.')
M(T, 'Stomach acid is an example of', 'a chemical barrier', ['a physical barrier', 'adaptive immunity', 'a vaccine'], 'Low pH kills many swallowed microbes.')
M(T, 'Histamine is released by', 'mast cells during inflammation', ['red blood cells', 'B cells only', 'the liver'], 'It dilates vessels and makes them leaky.')
W(T, 2, 'Describe one physical and one chemical barrier in humans.', ['Physical: intact skin blocks pathogens from entering the body.', 'Chemical: stomach acid (low pH) kills many pathogens that are swallowed.'],
  [('One correct physical AND one chemical barrier, described', 2), ('One barrier', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Outline how plants respond to a pathogen once it has entered.', ['Infected cells may die quickly (hypersensitive response) so the pathogen cannot spread, and the area can be sealed with lignin or callose.', 'Plants also make antimicrobial chemicals such as phytoalexins.'],
  [('Two correct responses', 2), ('One response', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how inflammation helps the body respond to a pathogen.', ['Damaged cells and mast cells release histamine.', 'Histamine dilates blood vessels so more blood flows to the area (redness, heat).', 'Capillaries become more permeable, so fluid, clotting factors and phagocytes move into the tissue (swelling).', 'Phagocytes engulf and digest the pathogens, and clotting helps wall off the infection, stopping it spreading.'],
  [('Explains the sequence from histamine to vessel changes to phagocytes destroying pathogens', 4), ('Describes several stages with some links', 3), ('Describes features of inflammation', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Immunity'
M(T, 'Antibodies are produced by', 'plasma cells (from B lymphocytes)', ['T helper cells', 'phagocytes', 'red blood cells'], 'Plasma cells secrete antibodies into blood and lymph.')
M(T, 'Cytotoxic (killer) T cells', 'destroy body cells infected by viruses', ['make antibodies', 'engulf bacteria', 'produce histamine'], 'Cell-mediated immunity.')
M(T, 'An antigen is', 'a molecule that triggers an immune response', ['a white blood cell', 'a type of antibody', 'a pathogen\'s DNA only'], 'Often proteins on the pathogen surface.')
M(T, 'The secondary immune response is faster because of', 'memory cells', ['phagocytes', 'mucus', 'fever'], 'Memory B and T cells recognise the antigen quickly.')
M(T, 'Helper T cells', 'activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells', ['make antibodies', 'kill infected cells', 'carry oxygen'], 'They release cytokines to coordinate the response.')
M(T, 'Passive immunity is gained by', 'receiving antibodies made by another organism', ['being vaccinated', 'catching the disease', 'making memory cells'], 'e.g. breast milk or an antivenom.')
M(T, 'A vaccine works by', 'exposing the body to antigens so memory cells form', ['killing pathogens directly', 'giving antibodies to the patient', 'raising body temperature'], 'This gives artificial active immunity.')
M(T, 'Each antibody is specific because', 'its binding site matches one antigen shape', ['it is made by any cell', 'it binds all pathogens', 'it is made of lipid'], 'Complementary shapes, like a lock and key.')
M(T, 'The innate immune system', 'responds the same way to every pathogen', ['remembers each pathogen', 'only uses antibodies', 'takes weeks to start'], 'It is fast and non-specific.')
M(T, 'Antigen-presenting cells', 'display pieces of pathogen on their surface to T cells', ['make antibodies', 'kill infected cells', 'store memory'], 'e.g. macrophages and dendritic cells.')
W(T, 2, 'Distinguish between active and passive immunity.', ['Active immunity: the body makes its own antibodies and memory cells after infection or vaccination, so it lasts long.', 'Passive immunity: ready-made antibodies are received (e.g. from breast milk or an injection), so protection is immediate but short-lived.'],
  [('Clear distinction including memory cells or duration', 2), ('Describes one type', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Outline the role of memory cells in immunity.', ['Memory B and T cells form during the first exposure and stay in the body for years.', 'On re-exposure they recognise the antigen and quickly multiply, giving a faster, larger response so the person does not become sick.'],
  [('Outlines formation and faster secondary response', 2), ('One correct point', 1)])
W(T, 5, 'Explain how the adaptive immune system responds to a viral infection.', ['Macrophages engulf viruses and present viral antigens to helper T cells.', 'Helper T cells are activated and release cytokines that activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells specific to that antigen.', 'Humoral response: B cells divide into plasma cells that make antibodies; antibodies bind viruses, neutralising them and marking them for phagocytes.', 'Cell-mediated response: cytotoxic T cells recognise and destroy body cells infected with the virus, stopping new viruses being made.', 'Memory B and T cells remain, so a later infection gives a faster, stronger secondary response.'],
  [('Explains antigen presentation, helper T cells, humoral and cell-mediated responses, and memory', 5), ('Explains most stages with links', 4), ('Describes humoral and cell-mediated responses', 3), ('Describes one response', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Prevention, Treatment and Control'
M(T, 'Antibiotics are effective against', 'bacteria', ['viruses', 'prions', 'all pathogens'], 'They target bacterial structures such as cell walls.')
M(T, 'Herd immunity occurs when', 'enough people are immune that a disease cannot spread easily', ['everyone has caught the disease', 'only animals are vaccinated', 'a drug is given to the whole population'], 'It protects people who cannot be vaccinated.')
M(T, 'Quarantine reduces the spread of disease by', 'separating people who may have been exposed', ['killing pathogens in water', 'making antibodies', 'curing the infected'], 'It breaks the chain of transmission.')
M(T, 'Antibiotic resistance increases mainly because of', 'overuse and misuse of antibiotics', ['vaccination', 'washing hands', 'quarantine'], 'Natural selection favours resistant bacteria.')
M(T, 'Antiviral drugs work by', 'interfering with virus replication', ['killing bacteria', 'making antibodies', 'breaking cell walls'], 'e.g. stopping viral entry or copying.')
M(T, 'Which is a public health measure?', 'Treating the water supply', ['Taking antibiotics for a cold', 'Using antivenom', 'Getting a blood test'], 'Clean water prevents waterborne diseases.')
M(T, 'Using plant compounds known to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as medicines, e.g. smoke bush, is an example of', 'traditional knowledge contributing to treatment', ['a vaccine', 'quarantine', 'genetic engineering'], 'Many modern medicines have traditional origins.')
M(T, 'Hand washing prevents disease by', 'removing pathogens before they reach the mouth or eyes', ['making antibodies', 'raising body temperature', 'killing all viruses in the body'], 'It is a simple, effective control measure.')
W(T, 2, 'Explain how vaccination programs can protect people who are not vaccinated.', ['When a high proportion of the population is immune, an infected person rarely meets someone susceptible.', 'So the pathogen cannot spread through the population (herd immunity), protecting those who cannot be vaccinated, such as babies.'],
  [('Explains herd immunity with how transmission is reduced', 2), ('Names herd immunity', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Outline how antibiotic resistance develops in a bacterial population.', ['Random mutations give a few bacteria resistance to an antibiotic.', 'When the antibiotic is used, susceptible bacteria die and resistant ones survive and reproduce, so resistance spreads (natural selection).'],
  [('Mutation plus selection by antibiotic use', 2), ('One correct point', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Evaluate the effectiveness of a named strategy used to control the spread of a disease.', ['Strategy: vaccination against measles.', 'The MMR vaccine gives immunity in about 97% of people after two doses, so most vaccinated people cannot catch or spread measles.', 'High coverage (around 95%) gives herd immunity; measles was eliminated from Australia in 2014.', 'Limitations: outbreaks occur where coverage falls or from imported cases; it needs cold storage and public trust.', 'Judgement: highly effective when coverage is high, so it is one of the most successful disease controls, but it depends on maintaining coverage.'],
  [('Describes the strategy, gives strengths AND limitations with evidence, and a judgement', 4), ('Strengths and limitations with implied judgement', 3), ('Describes the strategy and an effect', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Homeostasis'
M(T, 'Homeostasis is', 'keeping the internal environment relatively stable', ['the growth of an organism', 'the response to every stimulus', 'the body temperature of 37 °C only'], 'Variables such as temperature and glucose stay within narrow limits.')
M(T, 'Most homeostatic mechanisms work by', 'negative feedback', ['positive feedback', 'random change', 'mutation'], 'A change triggers a response that reverses it.')
M(T, 'When blood glucose rises, the pancreas releases', 'insulin', ['glucagon', 'adrenaline', 'ADH'], 'Insulin makes cells take up glucose and the liver store glycogen.')
M(T, 'Glucagon causes', 'the liver to break glycogen into glucose', ['cells to take up glucose', 'sweating', 'more urine'], 'It raises blood glucose.')
M(T, 'When body temperature rises, blood vessels in the skin', 'dilate (vasodilation)', ['constrict', 'stop blood flow', 'release insulin'], 'More heat is lost by radiation.')
M(T, 'The control centre for body temperature is the', 'hypothalamus', ['pancreas', 'kidney', 'skin'], 'It receives receptor signals and coordinates responses.')
M(T, 'Shivering helps raise body temperature because', 'muscle contractions release heat', ['it reduces blood flow to muscles', 'it releases sweat', 'it lowers metabolism'], 'Respiration in muscles produces heat.')
M(T, 'ADH acts on the kidneys to', 'reabsorb more water', ['remove more water', 'remove glucose', 'make urine more dilute'], 'Released when the blood is too concentrated.')
M(T, 'Which is an adaptation of desert plants to reduce water loss?', 'Sunken stomata', ['Large thin leaves', 'Many stomata on the upper surface', 'No cuticle'], 'Humid air trapped near sunken stomata reduces transpiration.')
M(T, 'In a feedback loop, the receptor', 'detects a change in a variable', ['carries out the response', 'makes hormones', 'stores glucose'], 'Stimulus → receptor → control centre → effector → response.')
M(T, 'The endocrine system communicates by', 'hormones carried in the blood', ['electrical impulses in neurons', 'diffusion across synapses only', 'lymph fluid only'], 'Slower but longer lasting than nerves.')
W(T, 2, 'Outline the stimulus–response model using an example.', ['Stimulus: body temperature rises; receptors (thermoreceptors in skin and hypothalamus) detect it.', 'The hypothalamus (control centre) sends signals to effectors: sweat glands sweat and skin vessels dilate, so temperature falls (response).'],
  [('Correct sequence with an example, naming receptor, control centre and effector', 2), ('Partial sequence', 1)])
W(T, 2, 'Describe how the body responds to low blood glucose.', ['Alpha cells in the pancreas detect low glucose and release glucagon.', 'Glucagon makes the liver break glycogen into glucose and release it into the blood, raising glucose back to normal.'],
  [('Glucagon released and liver glycogen broken down', 2), ('One correct point', 1)])
W(T, 4, 'Explain how negative feedback maintains blood glucose levels after a meal.', ['After a meal glucose is absorbed, so blood glucose rises above the normal range.', 'Beta cells in the pancreas detect the rise and release insulin.', 'Insulin makes body cells take up glucose and makes the liver and muscles convert glucose to glycogen.', 'Blood glucose falls back to normal, and as it falls insulin release decreases. The response reverses the change, which is negative feedback.'],
  [('Explains the rise, detection, insulin action and reversal as negative feedback', 4), ('Describes insulin\'s role with most steps', 3), ('Describes insulin release or action', 2), ('Relevant point', 1)])

T = 'Causes and Effects'
